 Europe","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"","title":"West comes East","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"","title":"The reform movements","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"","title":"Industry, constitutions, and nationalism","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"","title":"Rise of the secular modernists","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"","title":"The crisis of modernity","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"","title":"The tide turns.","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}}],"covers":[6463756],"local_id":["urn:sfpl:31223089508510","urn:sfpl:31223089508536","urn:sfpl:31223089508544","urn:sfpl:31223089508569","urn:sfpl:31223089508577","urn:sfpl:31223116642944","urn:sfpl:31223082678344","urn:sfpl:31223089508627","urn:sfpl:31223089508650","urn:sfpl:31223089508676","urn:sfpl:31223089508692","urn:sfpl:31223089508700","urn:sfpl:31223089508726","urn:sfpl:31223089508734","urn:cst:10017015216","urn:evs:39663100251891","urn:nuls:31786102373682"],"lc_classifications":["DS35.63 .A57 2009","DS35.63.A57 2009"],"key":"/books/OL23218500M","authors":[{"key":"/authors/OL222244A"}],"subtitle":"a history of the world through Islamic eyes","publish_places":["New York"],"languages":[{"key":"/languages/eng"}],"pagination":"p. cm.","source_records":["marc:marc_loc_updates/v37.i18.records.utf8:10051919:1296","marc:marc_loc_updates/v37.i26.records.utf8:53138016:1296","marc:marc_loc_updates/v37.i31.records.utf8:13265182:1300","marc:marc_loc_updates/v37.i32.records.utf8:10576967:1300","marc:marc_loc_updates/v37.i48.records.utf8:10934682:1304","marc:marc_openlibraries_sanfranciscopubliclibrary/sfpl_chq_2018_12_24_run03.mrc:319353839:4122","marc:marc_claremont_school_theology/CSTMARC2_barcode.mrc:68443305:4618","marc:marc_evangelical_seminary/Evangelical_Seminary_20200728.mrc:49089820:1379","marc:marc_loc_2016/BooksAll.2016.part36.utf8:115384693:1304","marc:marc_claremont_school_theology/CSTMARC2_multibarcode.mrc:68476344:4618","bwb:9781586486068","marc:marc_columbia/Columbia-extract-20221130-015.mrc:75649984:3087","marc:marc_nuls/NULS_PHC_180925.mrc:246272588:2037","marc:harvard_bibliographic_metadata/ab.bib.11.20150123.full.mrc:871518070:3049"],"title":"Destiny disrupted","dewey_decimal_class":["909/.09767"],"notes":{"type":"/type/text","value":"Includes bibliographical references and index."},"identifiers":{"librarything":["8095754"],"goodreads":["6297313"]},"isbn_13":["9781586486068"],"edition_name":"1st ed.","lccn":["2009017698"],"subjects":["Islam -- History","Civilization, Islamic -- History","Islamic countries -- History","Islamic Empire -- History","East and West","World history"],"publish_date":"2009","publish_country":"nyu","by_statement":"Tamim Ansary.","works":[{"key":"/works/OL1856493W"}],"type":{"key":"/type/edition"},"oclc_numbers":["246894282"],"number_of_pages":390,"description":{"type":"/type/text","value":"\"We in the West share a common narrative of world history: It runs from ancient Egypt and Mesopotamia, through Greece and Rome, through the rise of Christianity and the Dark Ages, through the Renaissance, the Reformation, and the Enlightenment, to industrialism, the secular state, and the triumph of democracy. As a young history buff growing up in Muslim Afghanistan, Tamim Ansary absorbed this narrative but also knew that it sidelined an entire civilization, one that had seen itself at the center of the global narrative for over a thousand years.\"\n\n\"In Destiny Disrupted, Ansary tells the rich story of world history as it looks from that other perspective. With the evolution of the Muslim community at the center, his story moves from the lifetime of Mohammed through a succession of far-flung empires, to the struggles and ideological movements that have wracked the Muslim world in recent centuries, to the tangle of modern conflicts that culminated in the events of 9/11. He introduces the key people, events, ideas, legends, religious disputes, and turning points of world history from that other perspective, recounting not only what happened but how those events were interpreted and understood in that framework. He clarifies why these two great civilizations grew up oblivious to each other, what happened when they intersected, and how the Islamic world was affected by its slow recognition that Europe - a place it long perceived as primitive - had somehow hijacked destiny.\"--BOOK JACKET."},"latest_revision":18,"revision":18,"created":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2009-05-16T10:45:42.984621"},"last_modified":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2023-11-30T17:02:02.530824"}}]}